{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310783,"nonce":"WkX+n+PQLu/u1WU0exzPvTQjVPU=","title":"deltablue's 48x loss is ONE blocked call - the first virtual dispatch waits ~1.1s on the vtable registry while background promotion writes ...","message":"**DIAGNOSED 2026-08-21 by differential measurement.** The chain of wrong\nhypotheses matters as much as the answer — each was killed by a measurement:\n\n1. \"deltablue loses on allocation\" — NO: its peak memory is FLAT across 10 vs\n   40 iterations (BinaryTrees is the allocation bug, a different one).\n2. \"virtual dispatch costs ~1400ns/call\" — NO: that was the FIRST measurement\n   at N=3M; at N=30M the average fell 473→89 ns/call. Fixed cost amortising.\n3. \"the registry slow path / RwLock+HashMap per call\" — NO: counters show\n   slow_path=0, flat_rebuilds=1; the flat fast path is hit every call.\n4. \"LLVM background compile contention\" — NO: Cranelift-only shows the\n   IDENTICAL ~1.3s inside-total.\n\n**PROVEN: ns_max_single = 1,136,101,041.** ONE dispatch call took 1.136s —\n94% of all time ever spent inside haxe_vtable_lookup. The first virtual\ndispatch blocks in `ensure_flat_vtable` → `VTABLE_REGISTRY.read()` while\nbackground tier promotion streams `haxe_vtable_set_slot`/register writes\n(each register also NULLS the flat table — an invalidation storm).\nSteady-state virtual dispatch is ~46ns (closure calls through the same\nCallIndirect lowering: 1ns — so the remaining 45ns is the FFI hop +\ndouble-load, a separate smaller item).\n\n**THE TELL that should have given it away instantly: deltablue's CI Execute\ntimes across ALL FIVE rayzor tiers are 603/606/603/596/603 ms — within 1.6%.\nCompute never lines up across interpreter-warmup/Cranelift/LLVM like that; a\nfixed startup block does.** deltablue's real work is ~12-22ms (hxcpp/HL); the\n~600ms is the wait. Fibonacci/mandelbrot/nbody don't block because they make\nno virtual calls.\n\n**FIXED 6304c3e3 — and the lock/RCU theory above was WRONG. TWO defects:**\n1. **The freeze densified over the LARGEST type_id, and one id is\n   POINTER-SIZED (347,876,344)** ⇒ a ~348M-entry table, gigabytes\n   zero-faulted inside the first dispatch. deltablue peak 8.19 GB → 32.7 MB;\n   `rayzor run` 2.9s → 0.26s. Dense cap 2^16 + frozen sparse map for outliers.\n2. **install_beadie's wait_for_drain(1s) can NEVER drain** — `main` holds the\n   execution counter until exit — so every promotion attempt burned the full\n   1s with state=PromotionRequested, blocking anything entering\n   wait_and_enter_execution. Removed: a one-pointer install is already\n   serialized by the RwLock and old code is Box::leak'd. Runner deltablue\n   1111-1470ms → **88ms**.\n\n**THE TRAP THAT COST A REVERT: the two defects live on DIFFERENT ENTRY POINTS**\n((1) `rayzor run`, (2) TieredBackend/benchmark_runner). Fixing (2) and testing\non (1) showed nothing ⇒ wrongly reverted, later re-applied. Always test a\ntiered-backend change through the RUNNER, not `rayzor run`.\n\n**STILL OPEN:** the pointer-sized type_id's provenance (id-space pun in\nregister/header path — [[bugs_stdtypes_type_addition_breaks_nue]] family);\nsteady dispatch ~60ns vs closure 1ns (FFI hop); deltablue 88ms vs hxcpp 12ms\n= the malloc-per-new allocator (BinaryTrees isolates it at 6.4x).\n\n**Instruments (committed):** `RAYZOR_VTABLE_STATS=1` prints calls/slow_path/\nflat_rebuilds/ns_total/ns_avg/ns_max_single per million calls + freeze\nread-lock wait. Probes: /tmp/dispatch3.hx (select vs non-overridden vs\noverridden), /tmp/dispatch4.hx (closure vs virtual — closure is the control\nthat proves CallIndirect itself is fine).\n\n**Measurement traps hit in THIS investigation:** `$?` after a pipeline is the\npipeline's exit, not rayzor's (use `\u003e f 2\u003e\u00261; echo $?`); a single N is\nmeaningless when a fixed cost is in play — vary N and fit; `sample` misses\nshort-lived or JIT-frame processes; first reading (1378ns) was 3x off from\ncontention with my own builds.\n\nRelated: [[bugs_published_bench_lost_and_faked_rows]] (the tier-uniform ~600ms\nwas visible there and unremarked), [[perf_mandelbrot_45x_tierup_regression]],\n[[reference_hashlink_bench_arm64_limits]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_deltablue_is_a_startup_block_not_dispatch` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}